markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Getting Data Online


#### When your code asks for data from the internet, it doesn’t get the answer instantly. Just like ordering something online, there’s a waiting period before you know what happened.


---

<img src="https://images.code.org/c0123c345ec6c97b12ecfd2c29f89d47-image-1771349104095.png" alt="icon of an open mailbox with a package inside" width="100px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 100px 0px 20px;">

## Scenario: The Mystery Mailbox

#### Imagine you ordered a **mystery box** online.  
#### You walk to the mailbox to check it.


<div style="clear:both;"></div>


> #### **Question:** What could be happening with your package right now?



---

## Delivery Status Chart

<div style="border:1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius:12px; overflow:hidden; margin:12px 0; max-width:760px;">
  <div style="background:#f9fafb; padding:10px 12px; border-bottom:1px solid #e5e7eb;font-size: 14pt;">
    <strong>Mailbox Delivery States</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="padding:12px;">
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size: 14pt;;">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px; border-bottom:1px solid #e5e7eb;">State</th>
          <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px; border-bottom:1px solid #e5e7eb;">What it looks like in the mailbox story</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:10px; border-bottom:1px solid #f0f2f5;">
            <span style="display:inline-block; padding:4px 10px; border-radius:999px; background:#dccbea; font-size: 14pt;">
              <strong>Pending</strong>
            </span>
          </td>
          <td style="padding:10px; border-bottom:1px solid #f0f2f5; font-size: 14pt;">
            Still waiting — you haven’t opened it yet, so you don’t know what’s inside.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:10px; border-bottom:1px solid #f0f2f5;font-size: 14pt;">
            <span style="display:inline-block; padding:4px 10px; border-radius:999px; background:#b2dee3; font-size: 14pt;">
              <strong>Fulfilled</strong>
            </span>
          </td>
          <td style="padding:10px; border-bottom:1px solid #f0f2f5;font-size: 14pt;">
            The package arrives safely and is in good condition.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:10px;">
            <span style="display:inline-block; padding:4px 10px; border-radius:999px; background:#f7bfbf; font-size: 14pt;">
              <strong>Rejected</strong>
            </span>
          </td>
          <td style="padding:10px;font-size: 14pt;">
            The package was lost/damaged, or you got a delivery failure note.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

---

## Match the State (Dropdown)

<div style="border:1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius:12px; padding:12px; background:#ffffff; max-width:760px;">
  <div style="margin-bottom:8px;font-size: 14pt;">
    <strong>Prompt:</strong> Which state matches the moment when they are <em>walking back from the mailbox</em> (before looking inside)?
  </div>

  
  <details style="margin:10px 0; border:1px solid #dbeafe; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; background:#eff6ff;">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer; font-weight:700;font-size: 14pt;">Show Answer ✅</summary>
    <div style="padding-top:10px; line-height:1.55;">
      <div style="font-size: 14pt;"><strong>Answer: Pending</strong></div>
      <div style="margin-top:6px;font-size: 14pt;">
        Even if the delivery process has started, the outcome isn’t known yet.
        Until you actually see the package (or a note), the delivery is still <strong>pending</strong>.
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
</div>

---

## Connection to JavaScript Promises

<p style = "font-size: 14pt;">These same three words — <strong>pending</strong>, <strong>fulfilled</strong>, and <strong>rejected</strong> — are exactly how JavaScript describes the status of a <strong>Promise</strong>.</p>

<div style="display:grid; gap:10px; margin:12px 0; max-width:760px;">
  <div style="border:1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius:12px; padding:12px; background:#ffffff;font-size: 14pt;">
    <div><strong>Pending</strong></div>
    <div style="margin-top:4px;font-size: 14pt;">The request is still in progress (you’re still waiting).</div>
  </div>
  <div style="border:1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius:12px; padding:12px; background:#ffffff;font-size: 14pt;">
    <div><strong>Fulfilled</strong></div>
    <div style="margin-top:4px;font-size: 14pt;">The request succeeded — your code received the data.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="border:1px solid #e5e7eb; border-radius:12px; padding:12px; background:#ffffff;font-size: 14pt;">
    <div><strong>Rejected</strong></div>
    <div style="margin-top:4px;font-size: 14pt;">Something went wrong — the request failed (error).</div>
  </div>
</div>



<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 A <strong>promise</strong> an object that represents a value that is not available yet but will be resolved in the future. It's a placeholder for the result of an asynchronous operation. <br><br> It has three states: <strong>pending, fulfilled, or rejected.</strong>

</p>
</div>


MARKDOWN
